1
00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:02.669
Welcome to this course on Spec-Driven Development

2
00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:04.437
built in partnership with JetBrains.

3
00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:08.041
Spec-Driven Development is currently the best type of workflow

4
00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:11.344
for building serious applications with agentic coding assistance.

5
00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:14.547
Give your coding agent a markdown file or a long prompt,

6
00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.649
explaining exactly what to build

7
00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:18.451
and it implements that spec.

8
00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:21.154
Rather than writing code by hand,

9
00:00:21.154 --> 00:00:22.622
you focus on writing down the

10
00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:24.357
context that the agent doesn't already have.

11
00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:27.861
I'm delighted that our instructor for this course is Paul Everett,

12
00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:29.863
who's developer advocate at JetBrains.

13
00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:32.866
Thank you Andrew and wait, I didn't know you wore

14
00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:35.068
spectacles, You're right.

15
00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:39.339
I don't actually need these. Okay, that's what I thought.

16
00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:41.841
Anyway, Spec-Driven Development

17
00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:44.644
has three main benefits that you'll start to see right away.

18
00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:47.347
First, you can control large code changes

19
00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:49.349
with small changes to the spec.

20
00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:53.286
One sentence like, use SQLite with Prisma ORM,

21
00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:55.588
might affect hundreds of lines of code.

22
00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:59.592
Change that to MongoDB for the same downstream amplification.

23
00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:02.762
This makes writing specs really efficient,

24
00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:05.265
far more so than writing code.

25
00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:08.468
Second, specs help eliminate context decay

26
00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:13.073
between sessions, preserving the non-negotiable.

27
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:17.343
Agents are stateless, so loading them with the highest quality context

28
00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:19.846
right when they boot up is important.

29
00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:24.250
And finally, specs improve your intent fidelity.

30
00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:29.222
You define the problem, success criteria, constraints, and so on,

31
00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:32.525
and the agent can elaborate to create a fuller plan.

32
00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:36.096
One way I often write a spec is by having a conversation

33
00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:40.300
with an agent like Claude Code or Gemini or ChatGPT Codex,

34
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.302
to make the key architectural choices

35
00:01:42.302 --> 00:01:45.905
using my knowledge of how I want to make different trade-offs.

36
00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:49.609
Then have the agent summarize the key decisions in a markdown file.

37
00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:52.178
Writing a spec requires thinking,

38
00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:53.847
and this is hard work.

39
00:01:54.047 --> 00:01:56.149
You have to decide what product you want to build,

40
00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:59.452
what is its features, its technical architecture.

41
00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:02.956
And without a spec, you'll be leaving these important decisions

42
00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:05.058
up to the whims of the coding agent,

43
00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.260
which might be okay if you want to

44
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.062
move really fast and just roll the dice,

45
00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:11.264
but certainly leads to less maintainable code

46
00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:13.666
and sometimes pretty weird products.

47
00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:17.837
For example, I've seen teams working on a complex software product

48
00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:20.206
where there was no clear spec,

49
00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:22.609
and this led to many downstream headaches

50
00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:26.713
from these different coding agents under the direction of different developers,

51
00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:29.215
building quickly, building in contradictory ways.

52
00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:32.785
Spec-Driven Development involves developing a constitution

53
00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:36.489
at the project level to define the immutable standards.

54
00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:39.993
Then iterating through feature development loops.

55
00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:42.295
These loops isolate each feature

56
00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:46.699
on its own branch with plan, implement, and verify steps

57
00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:49.402
that leave a clean slate between features

58
00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:52.338
and reduce headaches and context switching.

59
00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:56.943
This same workflow supports both greenfield and brownfield projects.

60
00:02:57.443 --> 00:03:00.146
In greenfield projects, you start from scratch.

61
00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:04.350
You'll develop the constitution in a conversation with the agent.

62
00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:07.453
In Brownfield, existing code bases,

63
00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:11.257
you'll generate the project constitution based on the existing code base.

64
00:03:11.658 --> 00:03:15.762
In both cases, you'll then iterate through these feature development loops,

65
00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:19.065
managing versioning in small steps.

66
00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:22.468
In this course, you'll also see how to write

67
00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:25.872
your own agent skills to automate your spec-driven workflow.

68
00:03:26.773 --> 00:03:29.475
You know, if you can accomplish what you need

69
00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:31.878
in just one short prompt, that's great.

70
00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:34.981
I'm definitely an advocate of lazy prompting when it works.

71
00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:38.284
But the great developers I know out there almost always

72
00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:42.388
will write detailed specs for projects with any significant complexity,

73
00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:45.091
because they have unique context and an opinion

74
00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:47.060
on what or how to build

75
00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.262
that'll be superior to letting the LLM,

76
00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:52.265
which is missing that context, pick randomly.

77
00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:54.634
If a coding agent is going to go

78
00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:56.669
off and write code for 20 or 30 minutes,

79
00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:00.673
which may correspond to several hours of traditional developer work,

80
00:04:01.074 --> 00:04:03.643
you're often better off sitting down for three or four minutes

81
00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:05.645
and writing really clear instructions.

82
00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:08.648
Many people have contributed to this course, including

83
00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:12.852
Konstantin Chaika and Zina Smirnova from JetBrains

84
00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:15.655
and Isabel Zaro from DeepLearning.AI.

85
00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:19.659
Let's go on to the next video and let's write some specs.
